Use the native Codex daemon in activated pods

Let Codex manage its app server, package updates, and remote control.
Bridge the host frontend to the native Unix socket so ordinary Codex
lifecycle and mobile pairing commands address the same instance.

Activate background startup only after rumpel codex is used in a pod.
Restore activated daemons through the existing container server setup,
without changing container entrypoints. Persist initial permission
defaults and retain the pod's credentials and config on reconnect. Keep
native lifecycle and state handling inside the pod server, with the
existing host proxy, credential upload and state-transfer protocol. Old
pods can retain their manual app server without host-side special cases.
Codex startup failures leave shell access available for repairs.

Provide procps where native process management needs it. Exclude runtime
artifacts from snapshots and reset mobile identity only when forking,
so a fork cannot impersonate the source pod.
